[{"id":1481216,"date":"2026-10-12T00:05:00","slug":"apod-2026-october-12-jupiter-and-the-moons","link":"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/image-article\/apod-2026-october-12-jupiter-and-the-moons\/","title":{"rendered":"APOD: 2026 October 12 &#8211; Jupiter and the Moons"},"content":{"rendered":"<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-secondary-navigation\"><div class=\"hds-secondary-navigation-wrapper z-top width-100 padding-0\"><div id=\"\" class=\"hds-secondary-navigation width-full border-bottom-1px text-center hds-color-mode-dark hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-hdsnav\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"hds-secondary-nav-mobile-button display-flex tablet:display-flex desktop:display-none width-full flex-align-center bg-carbon-90 border-color-carbon-80 color-spacesuit-white\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"hds-secondary-nav-track\"><span>APOD<\/span><svg width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" enable-background=\"new 0 0 400 400\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 400\" xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/2000\/svg\"><path d=\"m4.2 122.2 195.1 195.1 196.5-196.6-37.9-38-157.8 157.8-156.8-156.8z\" \/><\/svg><\/button><div class=\"hds-secondary-nav-track grid-container grid-container-extrawide padding-0 tablet:padding-x-3 desktop:padding-x-2 hds-secondary-navigation-has-breadcrumbs\"><div class=\"hds-secondary-navigation-breadcrumbs display-none tablet:display-none desktop:display-flex flex-align-center\"><nav class=\"usa-breadcrumb\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumbs\"><ol class=\"usa-breadcrumb__list\"><li class=\"usa-breadcrumb__list-item\"><a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/\" class=\"usa-breadcrumb__link\"><span>Science<\/span><\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-breadcrumb__list-item\"><a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/apod\/apod-index\/\" class=\"usa-breadcrumb__link\"><span>APOD<\/span><\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-breadcrumb__list-item usa-current\" aria-current=\"page\"><span>APOD: 2026 October 12 \u2013&#8230;<\/span><\/li><\/ol><\/nav><\/div><div class=\"hds-secondary-navigation-menu-items\"><nav><ul class=\"usa-nav__primary usa-accordion flex-justify-center margin-0\"><li class=\"usa-nav__primary-item \"><a class=\"usa-nav__link\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/apod\/\">Today&#8217;s APOD<\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-nav__primary-item \"><a class=\"usa-nav__link\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/apod\/archive\/\">Archive<\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-nav__primary-item \"><a class=\"usa-nav__link\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/apod\/submit\/\">Submissions<\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-nav__primary-item \"><a class=\"usa-nav__link\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/apod\/apod-index\/\">Index<\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-nav__primary-item \"><a class=\"usa-nav__link\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/apod\/apod-search\/\">Search<\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-nav__primary-item \"><a class=\"usa-nav__link\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/apod\/apod-calendar\/\">Calendar<\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-nav__primary-item \"><a class=\"usa-nav__link\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/feed\/apod-basic\/\">RSS<\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-nav__primary-item \"><a class=\"usa-nav__link\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/apod\/apod-education\/\">Education<\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-nav__primary-item \"><a class=\"usa-nav__link\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/apod\/apod-about\/\">About<\/a><\/li><li class=\"usa-nav__primary-item \"><a class=\"usa-nav__link\" href=\"https:\/\/asterisk.apod.com\/discuss_apod.php?date=261012\" rel=\"noopener\">Discuss<\/a><\/li><\/ul><\/nav><\/div><div class=\"hds-secondary-navigation-menu-spacer\">&nbsp;<\/div><\/div><\/div><\/div><\/div>\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media-detail-hero nasa-gb-align-wide width-full maxw-full margin-y-5 desktop:margin-y-7 padding-x-3 media-detail-hero--suppress-inner-media-caption hds-module alignwide wp-block-nasa-blocks-media-detail-hero\">\t<div class=\"grid-container grid-container-block padding-x-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"label margin-bottom-1\">APOD<\/p>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"heading-22 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-2\">Astronomy Picture of the Day<\/p>\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"p-md margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3 media-detail-hero__subtext\">\n\t\t\t\t<a href=\"\/apod\/archive\/\">Discover the cosmos!<\/a> Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\t\t\t<\/p>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"media-detail-hero__media margin-bottom-6\">\n\t\t\t\t<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-full\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-fit \"><a href=\"https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/content\/dam\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/JupiterMoons_Upreti_3105.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"In front of a dark background an image of Earth&#039;s Moon  appears with a lower crescent brightly lit. The rest of the Moon is also faintly visible. Above the Moon is a bright point of  light surrounded by four fainter points of light in row. Please see the explanation for more detailed information. (opens in a new tab)\"><img decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/JupiterMoons_Upreti_3105.jpg?w=0&amp;h=0&amp;fit=clip&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"In front of a dark background an image of Earth&#039;s Moon  appears with a lower crescent brightly lit. The rest of the Moon is also faintly visible. Above the Moon is a bright point of  light surrounded by four fainter points of light in row. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" \/><\/a><\/figure><\/div><\/div><\/div>\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<h1 class=\"display-48 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3\">Jupiter and the Moons<\/h1>\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-row media-detail-hero__content-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"p-md margin-0 media-detail-hero__description\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<strong>Explanation:<\/strong> How many moons can you see? The best answer is five: four orbiting <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/jupiter\/\">Jupiter<\/a> and <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/moon\/facts\/#h-how-the-moon-got-its-name\">Luna<\/a> orbiting Earth. This multi-moon picture was possible because <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/image-article\/apod-2023-november-30-artemis-1-flight-day-13\/\">Earth&#8217;s Moon<\/a> moved directly <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/image-article\/apod-2023-august-8-moon-meets-jupiter\/\">in front<\/a> of <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/jupiter\/\">Jupiter<\/a> last week. The <a href=\"https:\/\/www.instagram.com\/p\/DeKFXPOP1y1\/\" rel=\"noopener\">featured image<\/a> is a series of exposures near alignment taken from <a href=\"https:\/\/youtu.be\/91YWWW10suA\" rel=\"noopener\">Westchester<\/a>, <a href=\"https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/New_York_(state)\" rel=\"noopener\">New York<\/a>, <a href=\"https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/United_States\" rel=\"noopener\">USA<\/a>. The sunlit crescent of Earth&#8217;s Moon, on the lower left, is overexposed, while the Moon&#8217;s night side, on the upper right, is faintly illuminated by <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/image-article\/apod-2025-april-3-the-da-vinci-glow\/\">Earthshine<\/a>. Lined up <a href=\"https:\/\/www.reddit.com\/r\/aww\/comments\/b7s5sw\/came_home_from_work_the_other_day_to_find_all_5\/#lightbox\" rel=\"noopener\">nearly vertically<\/a> above our Moon, listed from bottom to top, are Jupiter&#8217;s bright <a href=\"https:\/\/www.nasa.gov\/history\/410-years-ago-galileo-discovers-jupiters-moons\/\" rel=\"noopener\">Galilean satellites<\/a>: Ganymede, Io, Callisto, and Europa. <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/image-article\/apod-2025-september-1-callisto-dirty-battered-iceball\/\">Callisto<\/a>, <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/image-article\/apod-2023-november-28-ganymede-from-juno\/\">Ganymede<\/a>, and <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/image-article\/apod-2026-january-20-io-in-true-color\/\">Io<\/a> are larger than Earth&#8217;s Moon, while <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/jupiter\/jupiter-moons\/europa\/\">Europa<\/a> is only slightly smaller. NASA&#8217;s robotic spacecraft <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/mission\/juno\/\">Juno<\/a> is currently orbiting <a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/image-article\/apod-2026-january-18-jupiter-from-the-webb-space-telescope\/\">Jupiter<\/a> and has made close passes near three of these moons.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow&#8217;s picture:<\/strong> stairway to heaven\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/p>\n\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"display-none desktop:display-block desktop:grid-col-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/div>\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-5 margin-top-3 desktop:margin-top-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<table class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-table width-full\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tbody>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">Date:<\/th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tOctober 12, 2026\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">Credit &amp; Copyright:<\/th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<a href=\"https:\/\/www.instagram.com\/chiragupreti\/\" rel=\"noopener\">Chirag Upreti<\/a>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">Authors &amp; editors:<\/th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">A service of:<\/th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<a href=\"https:\/\/science.gsfc.nasa.gov\/astrophysics\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASD<\/a> at <a href=\"https:\/\/www.nasa.gov\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA<\/a> \/ <a href=\"https:\/\/www.nasa.gov\/centers\/goddard\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GSFC<\/a>,<br><a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/learn\/about-science-activation\/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA Science Activation<\/a> &amp; <a href=\"https:\/\/www.mtu.edu\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Michigan Tech. U.<\/a>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/tbody>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/table>\n\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t<\/div>\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"nasa-gb-align-wide nasa-button-link padding-y-1 padding-x-0 hds-module alignwide wp-block-nasa-blocks-related-link\">\n\t\t\t<a href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/apod\/random-apod\/\" target=\"_self\" class=\"button-primary button-primary-md link-external-false\" aria-label=\"Random APOD Generator\">\n\t\t\t<span class=\"line-height-alt-1\">Random APOD Generator<\/span>\n\t\t\t<svg viewBox=\"0 0 32 32\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/2000\/svg\"><circle class=\"button-primary-circle\" cx=\"16\" cy=\"16\" r=\"16\"><\/circle><path d=\"M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z\" class=\"color-spacesuit-white\"><\/path><\/svg>\n\t\t<\/a>\n\t\n\t<\/div>\n\n\n<div style=\"height:1px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-module alignwide wp-block-smd-block-next-prev\">            <div class=\"smd-next-prev-buttons-titles\">\n            <a class=\"smd-next-prev__prev\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/image-article\/apod-2026-october-11-ice-fishing-for-cosmic-neutrinos\/\">\n                <div class=\"hds-next-prev-nav-arrow hds-next-prev-arrow-prev\">\n                    <svg version=\"1.1\" x=\"0px\" y=\"0px\" width=\"9px\" height=\"9px\" viewBox=\"0 0 9 9\"><path class=\"st0\" d=\"M3.5,4.5l3.7-3.6L6.3,0L1.8,4.5L6.3,9l0.9-0.9L3.5,4.5z\"><\/path><\/svg>\n                <\/div>\n                <div class=\"smd-next-prev-buttons-titles__content\">\n                    <div class=\"smd-next-prev-buttons-titles__prev-label\">\n                        Yesterday&#039;s Image                    <\/div>\n                    <div class=\"smd-next-prev-buttons-titles__prev\">\n                        APOD: 2026 October 11 &#8211; Ice Fishing for Cosmic Neutrinos                    <\/div>\n                <\/div>\n            <\/a>\n            <a class=\"smd-next-prev__next\" href=\"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/image-article\/apod-2026-october-13-desert-stairway-to-heaven\/\">\n                <div class=\"smd-next-prev-buttons-titles__content\">\n                    <div class=\"smd-next-prev-buttons-titles__next-label\">\n                        Tomorrow&#039;s Image                    <\/div>\n                    <div class=\"smd-next-prev-buttons-titles__next\">\n                        APOD: 2026 October 13 &#8211; Desert Stairway to Heaven                    <\/div>\n                <\/div>\n                <div class=\"hds-next-prev-nav-arrow hds-next-prev-arrow-next\">\n                    <svg version=\"1.1\" xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/2000\/svg\" x=\"0px\" y=\"0px\" width=\"9px\" height=\"9px\" viewBox=\"0 0 9 9\"><path class=\"st0\" d=\"M5.5,4.5L1.8,8.1L2.7,9l4.5-4.5L2.7,0L1.8,0.9L5.5,4.5z\"><\/path><\/svg>\n                <\/div>\n            <\/a>\n        <\/div>\n    <\/div>\nExplanation: How many moons can you see? The best answer is five: four orbiting Jupiter and Luna orbiting Earth. This multi-moon picture was possible because Earth&#8217;s Moon moved directly in front of Jupiter last week. The featured image is a series of exposures near alignment taken from Westchester, New York, USA. The sunlit crescent of Earth&#8217;s Moon, on the lower left, is overexposed, while the Moon&#8217;s night side, on the upper right, is faintly illuminated by Earthshine. Lined up nearly vertically above our Moon, listed from bottom to top, are Jupiter&#8217;s bright Galilean satellites: Ganymede, Io, Callisto, and Europa. Callisto, Ganymede, and Io are larger than Earth&#8217;s Moon, while Europa is only slightly smaller. NASA&#8217;s robotic spacecraft Juno is currently orbiting Jupiter and has made close passes near three of these moons.Tomorrow&#8217;s picture: stairway to heaven\nDate:\nCredit &#038; Copyright: Chirag Upreti\nAuthors &#038; editors: Robert Nemiroff (MTU) &#038; Jerry Bonnell (UMCP)\nA service of: ASD at NASA \/ GSFC,NASA Science Activation &#038; Michigan Tech. U.","protected":false},"featured_media":1481218,"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/image-article\/1481216","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/image-article"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/image-article"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/900"}],"version-history":[{"count":2,"href":"https:\/\/science.nasa.gov\/wp-json\/wp\/